# 🧠 Very Important Difference

```java
Student s;
```

does **not** create an object.

```java
Student s = new Student();
```

does create an object.

| Code                         | Meaning                             |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `Student`                    | Class/reference type                |
| `s`                          | Reference variable                  |
| `new Student()`              | Creates an object                   |
| `Student s;`                 | Declares reference                  |
| `Student s = new Student();` | Declares reference + creates object |

---

## 🎯 Interview Answer

> **A class in Java is a reference type that serves as a blueprint for creating objects. A variable declared using a class name is a reference variable, which can refer to an object of that class.**

### 🧠 Easy Memory Trick

Remember these three:

```text
Class       → Blueprint 📋
Object      → Actual thing 🏠
Reference   → Way to access the object 🏷️
```

And:

```java
Student s = new Student();
```

means:

> **`Student` = type → `s` = reference → `new Student()` = object creation**.
